@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli Command Line Options
@brief Options for controlling the test output at run time.

@details
The µTest++ framework offers flexible control over test-output verbosity,
allowing users to tailor the level of detail to their specific
requirements and workflow.

By default, the reporter presents detailed results only for failed test
cases, while successful test cases are summarised in a single line,
indicating the total number of passed and failed checks.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --reporter=tap one two

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal

    # Subtest: Check various conditions
        1..0
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

Options may also be set programmatically, on platforms without a command
line, through `runner_options`.

The µTest++ options come first. The parsing of the options ends at the
first argument that does not start with `--`, or at the first `--` alone;
that argument, and all the following ones, are left to the application,
uninterpreted, and are available from `runner::application_arguments()`.
The `--` itself is not passed on, and an option argument given
separately (`--reporter human`) belongs to its option. An unknown `--`
option before that point, an unknown reporter or colour mode, or an
option with a missing argument, is an error: it is reported on `stderr`
and on the console, no test is run, and the exit code is 2.

@section cli-reporter Reporter

By default, the µTest++ output follows the
[TAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_Anything_Protocol) rules.

The human-readable format of the initial releases is also available. It is
intended for the people who read the console output, while the TAP format is
intended for the integration with other tools.

If needed, more reporting formats (like JUnit, JSON, etc.) can be added.

To explicitly ask for a specific format:

- `--reporter=tap` – the default TAP reporter
- `--reporter=human` – the human-readable reporter

@section cli-colours Colours

The human reporter highlights the results in colour. By default, the
colours are used only when `stdout` is a terminal, which can be detected
only on POSIX platforms. To select them explicitly, as in the GNU tools:

- `--color=auto` – use colours only when the output is a terminal
  (the default)
- `--color=always`, or `--color` alone – always use colours, for example
  in the CI systems that render the ANSI escape sequences but do not
  provide a terminal
- `--color=never` – never use colours

As in the GNU tools, the value must be attached with `=`, and the
synonyms `yes` and `force` (for `always`), `no` and `none` (for
`never`), and `tty` and `if-tty` (for `auto`) are also accepted. For
compatibility with Catch2 and Boost UT, `--use-colour=<mode>` or
`--use-colour <mode>`, with `yes`, `no`, or `auto` (or any other value
of `--color`), is also accepted.
The TAP reporter never uses colours, and the output file never receives
them.

On platforms without a command line, the mode can be set in
`runner_options::colour`.

@section cli-verbosity Verbosity

By default, µTest++ displays a summary line for each passing test case and
full expectation details for each failing one.

To adjust the verbosity, the following command-line options are available:

- `--verbose` – display all expectations, regardless of outcome
- `--quiet` – display only the session totals and, with the TAP reporter,
  one result line per test suite; the failed checks are not displayed
- `--silent` – suppress all console output and return only the exit code

On platforms without a command line, the level can be set in
`runner_options::verbosity_level`.

The source code for these examples is in
`tests/sources/minimal/src/main.cpp`.

@subsection cli-verbosity-verbose Verbose mode

In verbose mode, the µTest++ reporter provides detailed output for every
expectation, irrespective of whether it passes or fails.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --verbose --reporter=tap one two

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal

    # Subtest: Check various conditions
        ok 1 - answer is 42
        ok 2 - condition is true
        1..2
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@subsection cli-verbosity-quiet Quiet mode

In quiet mode, the µTest++ reporter displays only the overall session
totals. The TAP reporter also displays one `ok`/`not ok` line per test
suite, with the plan counting them, so that the output remains a valid
TAP document, which the TAP consumers read as failed when a suite
failed.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --quiet --reporter=tap one two

TAP version 14
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }
1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

Quiet mode tells whether the run failed, not what failed: the failed
checks, their locations, and their test cases are not displayed. This is
by design, since displaying in full only the failed test cases is what
the default mode does. To learn what failed, run without `--quiet`, or
keep `--quiet` and add `--output-file` with the TAP reporter, whose file
always receives the complete report, whatever the verbosity:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --quiet --output-file=sample-test.tap
@endcode

With the human reporter, the output file is complete only in the default
and verbose modes, so in quiet mode neither the console nor the file
shows the failures.

When exceptions are not enabled and a failed requirement aborts the test
execution, the results of the aborted test case are displayed in quiet
mode as well, since the totals are never reached.

@subsection cli-verbosity-silent Silent mode

In silent mode, the µTest++ reporter suppresses all console output, and
the framework returns only the exit code; the output file, if any, is
still written.

@section cli-output-file Output File

- `--output-file=<path>` or `--output-file <path>` – write a copy of the
  report to a file

The file never receives colours. With the TAP reporter, it always receives
the complete report, whatever the verbosity, so it can be passed to other
tools; with the human reporter, it is complete only in the default and
verbose modes. If the file cannot be opened, the error is reported on
`stderr`, the report is written only to the console, and the exit code
is 2. On platforms without a command line, the path can be set in
`runner_options::output_file_path`.

@section cli-filter Filter

By default, all test suites and test cases are run. To run only some of
them, the `--filter` option selects them by name, as `--grep` does in
node-tap:

- `--filter=<pattern>` or `--filter <pattern>` – run only the suites and
  the test cases whose names match the patterns

The option may be repeated, one pattern per nesting level: the first
pattern applies to the suite names, the second to the names of the test
cases, and each further pattern to the names of the test cases nested one
level deeper. The levels without a pattern are not filtered; to filter
only the test cases, use `*` for the suites.

In a pattern, `*` matches any sequence of characters, including an empty
one, `?` matches any single character, and `|` separates alternatives. The
match is case-sensitive, and the pattern must cover the whole name; for
example, `Args` does not select a suite named `Args suite`, but `Args*`
and `Args*|*namespace*` do. Since the patterns usually contain wildcards,
they must be quoted, to prevent the shell from expanding them.

The suites and the test cases that are not selected are reported as
skipped, keep their numbers in the TAP plans, and their code is not run.
The top suite is never skipped as a whole, since its body is `main()`, but
its test cases are filtered like those of any other suite.

On platforms without a command line, the patterns can be set in
`runner_options::filter_patterns`.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --filter='*namespace*' one two

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Sample
...
ok 1 - Sample # { passed, 31 checks in 13 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

ok 2 - Args suite # SKIP filtered

# Subtest: Explicit namespace suite
...
ok 3 - Explicit namespace suite # { passed, 2 checks in 2 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

# Subtest: Implicit namespace suite
...
ok 4 - Implicit namespace suite # { passed, 2 checks in 2 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

1..4
# { total: 35 checks passed, 0 failed, in 17 test cases, 4 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

To run only the test cases named `Check one` of the same suites:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --filter='*namespace*' --filter='Check one' one two
...
# Subtest: Explicit namespace suite

    # Subtest: Check one
        1..0
    ok 1 - Check one # { passed, 1 check }

    ok 2 - Check two # SKIP filtered

    1..2
ok 3 - Explicit namespace suite # { passed, 1 check in 1 test case, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
...
1..4
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 2 test cases, 15 skipped, 4 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

The test cases of the top suite `Sample` are skipped too, since its name
does not match the first pattern.

@section cli-list-tests Listing the Test Cases

- `--list-tests`, or `--list` – list the test cases instead of running
  them, as in Catch2 and Boost UT

The suite bodies run, to create their test cases, but the test case
bodies do not, so no check is evaluated. The selected test cases (see the
filter above) are listed under the names of their suites; the nested test
cases are not listed, since they are created by the bodies of their
parents. The list is written to the console, unless the verbosity is
`silent`, and to the output file, if any, and the exit code is 0 (or 2
after an option error).

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --list-tests
All available test cases:
  Sample
    Check various conditions
    ...
...
@endcode

The header is `Matching test cases:` when there are filter patterns. On
platforms without a command line, the listing can be requested with
`runner_options::list_tests`, and checked with `runner::is_listing()`.
